The context report identifies the three requested low-severity findings in [src/petty.ts](</Users/andrew/dev/crimes/evals/fixtures/01-messy-ts-app/src/petty.ts>).

1. `option_bag_junk_drawer` — `describeCheckout`

Although `CheckoutOptions` is named, it still combines six unrelated values: subscription, actor, locale, status, and retry state. An agent adding another checkout concern may append another property and another position in the serialized string without understanding its contract.

Refactor into cohesive records and destructure the owned fields:

```ts
interface CheckoutDescription {
  subscription: {
    plan: string;
    role: string;
  };
  locale: {
    region: string;
    currency: string;
  };
  execution: {
    status: string;
    shouldRetry: boolean;
  };
}

export function describeCheckout({
  subscription: { plan, role },
  locale: { region, currency },
  execution: { status, shouldRetry },
}: CheckoutDescription): string {
  return [
    plan,
    role,
    region,
    currency,
    status,
    String(shouldRetry),
  ].join(":");
}
```

This ties each new field to a specific concern and makes changes to the output ordering deliberate.

2. `return_shape_roulette` — `resolvePaymentResult`

The three branches have almost unrelated shapes, including one with no `ok` property. An agent could assume `result.ok` always exists, read `value` without narrowing, or add a fourth shape that callers silently overlook.

Use an explicit discriminated union:

```ts
type PaymentResult =
  | {
      kind: "error";
      code?: string;
      message?: string;
    }
  | {
      kind: "redirect";
      url?: string;
      permanent: boolean;
    }
  | {
      kind: "success";
      value?: string;
      cached: boolean;
    };

export function resolvePaymentResult(input: PaymentInput): PaymentResult {
  if (input.error) {
    return { kind: "error", code: input.code, message: input.message };
  }

  if (input.redirect) {
    return { kind: "redirect", url: input.url, permanent: false };
  }

  return { kind: "success", value: input.value, cached: false };
}
```

Callers can now switch on `kind`, and exhaustive checking exposes newly added variants.

3. `negative_flag_maze` — `canRunBilling`

`!disableBilling && !skipRetry` requires mentally negating both inputs. An agent extending it with another negative flag could easily invert one operand or choose the wrong `&&`/`||` combination.

Rename the inputs positively and return the predicate directly:

```ts
export function canRunBilling(
  isBillingEnabled: boolean,
  isRetryAllowed: boolean,
): boolean {
  return isBillingEnabled && isRetryAllowed;
}
```

The inversion should happen once at the boundary where legacy flags enter:

```ts
canRunBilling(!disableBilling, !skipRetry);
```

This also resolves the related `boolean_naming_drift` finding for those parameters. Tests should cover all four boolean combinations during the rename, since incorrect inversion at call sites is the main migration risk.
